  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Recent preprints validate biological sequence models on selection, calibration, and homology-controlled benchmarks rather than accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether learned biological sequence representations remain trustworthy once confounds are controlled. The antimicrobial peptide preprint enforces homology separation and length matching in its evaluation protocol, and reports that length alone separates classes. The protein language model benchmark compares 12 embedding models against MMseqs2 and Foldseek for remote-homology retrieval and finds that severe anisotropy degrades performance. MicroDUET adapts the ProkBERT genomic language model with phylogeny and function adapter heads to produce compact embeddings for pathogen surveillance. MADM and Calpha extend the scope of architecture design: MADM combines per-residue structural descriptors with PLM embeddings across eight antibody developability assays, while Calpha routes LLM residue states to a SimpleFold structure head for single-chain folding. The new element is explicit scrutiny of what these models exploit and what they are validated against, rather than headline accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
